NMOS Crosspoint — project rules

This is a Node + Svelte application for routing NMOS (IS-04/IS-05) media streams.
Match the conventions below; they describe the code as it actually is, not an ideal.

## Stack (do not assume otherwise)

- UI: Svelte 4 (`^4.2.8`) + Vite 5 + TypeScript, client-side only.
- There is NO SvelteKit. No SSR, no SSG, no file-based routing, no load functions,
  no form actions, no `+page`/`+error` files, no `$lib` alias, no adapters.
- There are NO Svelte 5 runes. Do not write `$state`, `$derived`, `$props`,
  `$effect`, or `$bindable`. Svelte 4 has none of them.
- Styling is Tailwind 3 + daisyUI 4. There is no Shadcn.
- No i18n library. No Paraglide.
- Server: TypeScript compiled to CommonJS, run as plain Node.

## Repo layout

- `ui/` — Vite app. `npm run build` outputs into `../server/public` with
  `emptyOutDir: true`, so the UI build writes into the server tree.
- `server/` — TypeScript service. Entry `server/src/server.ts`, compiled to
  `server/dist/server.js`.
- `docs/` — protocol and setup docs. `monitoring/` — Prometheus/Grafana provisioning.
- Config lives in `server/config/*.json`, seeded by copying `server/config_example/`.
  Runtime state is separate, under `server/state/`.

## UI conventions

- `<script lang="ts">` in every `.svelte` file.
- Props are Svelte 4 `export let`, with a default and usually an explicit type:
  `export let deviceId:string="";`
- Reactivity is `$:` statements. Force list re-render by reassigning:
  `uiList = [...uiList]`.
- Component communication: `createEventDispatcher` + `on:someEvent={(e)=>...}`
  reading `e.detail`; parents reach into children with `bind:this` and call methods.
- Svelte stores are effectively unused — only `topology.svelte` imports `writable`,
  and only because `@xyflow/svelte` requires it. Shared state lives on singleton
  services exposing RxJS `Subject`s. Follow that, not stores.
- No `<style>` blocks. There are none in the codebase. All CSS is global SCSS under
  `ui/src/scss/`, imported from `ui/src/app.scss`. Add page styles as a new file there.
- Markup mixes daisyUI/Tailwind utility classes with project class names.
  `tailwind.config.cjs` only scans `./src/routes/**` plus `safelist.txt` — a utility
  class used solely inside `ui/src/lib/` will be purged unless it is safelisted.
- Theming: `document.documentElement.setAttribute('data-theme', 'dark'|'light')`,
  persisted to `localStorage` under `nmos_crosspoint_dark_mode`.

### Routing

`svelte-routing`, declared inline in `ui/src/App.svelte`:

```svelte
<Router>
  <Route path="/details" component={Details}/>
  <Route path="/crosspoint"><Crosspoint bind:this={c} autoTake={autoTake}/></Route>
  <Route><h2>404 : Not found</h2></Route>
</Router>
```

Use the slot form when the route component needs props or bindings. Nav entries come
from a `let routes = [{label, icon, link}]` array rendered with `{#each}` + `<Link>`.

### File naming (differs per directory — preserve it)

- `ui/src/routes/` — all lowercase: `crosspoint.svelte`, `mediadevices/matroxcip.svelte`.
- `ui/src/lib/` — PascalCase components and services: `SetupDevice.svelte`,
  `ServerConnector/ServerConnectorService.ts`. Plain helpers stay lowercase (`functions.ts`).
- Imports alias to PascalCase regardless: `import Matroxcip from './routes/mediadevices/matroxcip.svelte'`.
- Paths are always relative. There is no path alias.

### Talking to the server

One websocket client: `ui/src/lib/ServerConnector/ServerConnectorService.ts`, a
module-scope singleton (`class _ServerConnector`, exported as a default instance).
State channels are patched with rfc6902. API: `sync(channel, id = 0): Subject<any>`,
`unsync(channel, id = 0)`, `get(route)`, `post(route, data)`, `addFeedback({level, message})`,
`startLoad()`/`endLoad()`.

Subscribe in `onMount`, and in `onDestroy` both unsubscribe AND `unsync` — missing the
`unsync` leaks a server-side ref count:

```ts
let sync:Subject<any>;
onMount(async () => {
  sync = ServerConnector.sync("crosspoint");
  sync.subscribe((obj:any)=>{ sourceState = obj; doFilter(); });
});
onDestroy(() => {
  sync.unsubscribe();
  ServerConnector.unsync("crosspoint");
});
```

Channels: `uiconfig`, `crosspoint`, `nmos`, `topology`, `log`, `connectionState`,
`mediadevices`, `mediadevmatroxcip`, `mediadevriedelembrionix`.

## Server conventions

- Singletons: a `static instance` assigned in the constructor
  (`NmosRegistryConnector.instance = this`). Some newer classes use
  `private static instance` + `static getInstance()`. Follow whichever the file uses.
- Filenames are camelCase, exported classes PascalCase: `nmosConnector.ts` →
  `NmosRegistryConnector`. Directories are camelCase except `lib/SyncServer/`.
- Expose state to the UI as a `SyncObject` field registered by route and permission:
  `server.addSyncObject("mediadevices","global",this.syncDeviceList)`.
  Register request handlers with `server.addRoute("POST","makeconnection","global",(client,query,postData)=>{...})`.
- Logging is `SyncLog` (streams to the UI `log` channel). All static, all
  `(topic, text, raw = null)`, returning a numeric log id:
  `SyncLog.error/warning/info/debug/verbose(topic, text, raw?)`, or the explicit
  `SyncLog.log(severity, topic, text, raw?)` with severity
  `"error" | "warning" | "info" | "debug" | "verbose"`. `debug`/`verbose` are dropped
  unless debug logging is enabled. Both call styles are in use; drivers favour `log()`.
- Config: synchronous `fs.readFileSync` + `JSON.parse` in a try/catch, then
  `parseSettings()` (`lib/parseSettings.ts`) applies defaults by mutating the object.
  There is no schema library and no typed Settings interface; settings are `any`.
  New options belong in `parseSettings` with a default, plus `config_example`.

### Plugin drivers

- `server/src/mediaDevices/` is auto-discovered: `lib/mediaDevices.ts` reads
  `./dist/mediaDevices/` and loads every `.js`. Adding a file registers a driver.
  Modules can be disabled via `settings.disabledModules.mediadevices`.
- `server/src/networkDevices/` is loaded by config `type` and each module must export
  a class named exactly `NIC_MOD` — see `networkDevice.ts`.

### Worker thread constraint

`lib/crosspointUpdateThread.ts` runs in a `worker_threads` Worker spawned from
`lib/crosspointAbstraction.ts` as the compiled `dist/.../crosspointUpdateThread.js`.

In that file, and anything it imports:
- `SyncLog` is unavailable (no instance, no websocket server). Log by posting a message
  for the host to re-log:
  `parentPort.postMessage(JSON.stringify({log:{severity:"warning",topic:"...",text:"...",raw:null}}))`
- Do not import `nmosConnector` or other singleton-dependent modules. Keep to pure
  helpers (`functions.ts`, `parseSettings`, types). Shared helpers used by the worker
  must stay dependency-free.
- All host/worker messages are JSON strings, not structure-cloned objects.

## Code style

Match the surrounding file rather than reformatting it.

- 4 spaces in `server/src` and in `.svelte` `<script>` blocks; 2 in `ui/src/main.ts`
  and Vite config.
- Double quotes dominate in server code; some newer UI files use single.
- `let` is used almost everywhere, including for values never reassigned. `const` mostly
  for module-level `require`s and newer code.
- `any` is used liberally at boundaries. `server/tsconfig.json` is NOT strict — no
  `strict`, no `strictNullChecks`, implicit `any` allowed. `ui/tsconfig.json` extends
  `@tsconfig/svelte` and IS strict.
- Server files mix ES `import` with CommonJS `const fs = require("fs")`.
- Errors are usually caught and reported via `SyncLog`, not rethrown; `LoggedError`
  carries a `logId` for the throw-with-log case. Retries are `setTimeout`/`setInterval`.
- Several files carry the header `/* NMOS Crosspoint / Copyright (C) 2021 Johannes Grieb */`.
  Preserve it.

## Build and verification

```
cd ui     && npm install && npm run build   # writes into ../server/public
cd server && npm install && npm run build   # tsc
cd server && npm run dev                    # tsc-watch + node --inspect
docker-compose up nmos-crosspoint-dev
```

There is no test framework, no linter, and no CI — `npm test` in `server/` fails by
design. Do not claim a change is verified by tests. The available checks are:

- `cd server && ./node_modules/.bin/tsc --noEmit`
- `cd ui && npm run check` (svelte-check)

For anything touching NMOS connection behaviour, note explicitly what still needs real
hardware to confirm; the device error bodies logged by `nmosConnector` are the oracle.

